Ported at commit 687f282e8625c6b8d11ff58211a90524be2e4317 (dsh-v0.2.1-rc.2, release dsh 0.2.1-rc.2). The files read
are listed with their SHA-256 (LF line endings) in source.json. The system prompt sections and their order, the
runtime-context, workspace-instruction, skill-catalog and time messages, the tool descriptions, parameter schemas,
argument checks, result and error texts, output limits, the read-before-write guard, the shell executors' environment
and UTF-8 handling, the repeat-call reminder, the request-series reasons and the balance-before-rate-limit reading come
from those files; they are marked "Ported from deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness" in src/dsh/ and test/unit/dsh/.

Three fixes of the later upstream f3803c96974ad4a94195fb97e5c637e9c2a3afe3 (dsh-v0.2.2-alpha.2) are ported on top,
with the pin unchanged: a leading `~` in a file path names the home folder (fs-local `expandHomePrefix`, ae0dfd1003 and
32004176bf), `glob` prunes unrelated folders when the pattern's folders are plain names (ef284800b9, e8bdbbe823,
b1c8c9bbbc, f9ef03834a) and `grep` takes an empty `include` as no filter (080a2c60b3, 4e4badb8f3). The files read
for them are listed under "backports" in source.json, and the code and tests cite "upstream f3803c9697".

The write-failure texts of the later upstream 66491b05e3f05ad35bcdd4f6cd7ed26041d9e3db (dsh-v0.2.2-rc.1) are ported
the same way: a write or edit the operating system refuses reads `cannot write "<path>": permission denied`, any other
failure it reports `cannot write "<path>": <its message>` (fsio `writeIoError`, 9b154c04a6 and 8f798da97d; tests
7fab3cf976). Their files are the second entry under "backports" in source.json, and the code and tests cite
"upstream 66491b05e3". No system prompt section, tool description or schema changed with either backport.

One deviation is recorded: since dsh-v0.2.2-alpha.3 upstream ships its fs-observation-policy disabled (fe5450b152,
14d3e53c19; upgrade guide docs/upgrade-guide/v0.2.2-alpha.2/fs-observation-policy-default-off), so dsh's write and edit
no longer need a prior read. own keeps the read-before-write guard in every variant, the reference variant included,
because it keeps own's parallel team agents and the user's own concurrent edits from being overwritten unseen
(decision DH-16, own/docs/13-dsh-harness.md section 3.5). The guard and its texts are dsh's own from the pin.

snapshots/ holds dsh's own snapshot files (system prompts, tool schemas, and message texts extracted from snapshot
session logs) that the tests compare against.

Nothing of dsh's DeepSeek adapter wire extensions or telemetry is ported: no `dsh_session_log` or other dsh-specific
request fields, and no session-log or OpenTelemetry upload.
